This is your DeepL v2 translation service on port 4500 (Node 18/Express + Redis 7.0), so here is the pattern that fits what you already have, with the fast path first.

## 1. Cache-first (Redis), then batch, then call

```js
const axios = require('axios');
const http = require('http'); const https = require('https');
const Redis = require('ioredis');
const redis = new Redis({ host: 'redis', port: 6379 });

// Reuse sockets — avoids a TLS handshake per translation
const client = axios.create({
  baseURL: 'https://api.deepl.com/v2',
  timeout: 2000,
  httpAgent: new http.Agent({ keepAlive: true, maxSockets: 50 }),
  httpsAgent: new https.Agent({ keepAlive: true, maxSockets: 50 }),
  headers: { Authorization: `DeepL-Auth-Key ${process.env.DEEPL_KEY}` },
});

const key = (t, s, tgt) => `tr:${s}:${tgt}:${require('crypto').createHash('sha1').update(t).digest('hex')}`;
const TTL = 900; // 15 min, same TTL you already use for recent translations

// DeepL v2 accepts repeated `text` params — one call for many strings
async function translateBatch(texts, sourceLang, targetLang) {
  const keys = texts.map(t => key(t, sourceLang, targetLang));
  const cached = await redis.mget(keys);          // single round-trip
  const misses = [];
  cached.forEach((v, i) => { v ? metrics.hit() : (metrics.miss(), misses.push(i)); });
  if (misses.length === 0) return cached;

  const params = new URLSearchParams();
  misses.forEach(i => params.append('text', texts[i]));
  params.append('source_lang', sourceLang.toUpperCase());
  params.append('target_lang', targetLang.toUpperCase());

  const res = await callWithRetry(params);         // see §2
  const pipe = redis.pipeline();
  res.translations.forEach((tr, n) => {
    const i = misses[n];
    cached[i] = tr.text;
    pipe.set(keys[i], tr.text, 'EX', TTL);
  });
  pipe.exec().catch(e => console.error('cache write failed', e.message)); // don't await
  return cached;
}
```

Batching matters for you specifically: DeepL's 5000 requests/day cap is per request, not per string, so batching stretches the quota as well as cutting latency. If Redis is unreachable, wrap the `mget` in try/catch and fall through to a direct API call — degraded, not broken.

For a single user message, don't batch-wait; call directly and run language detection (port 4000) and the GPT-4 call (port 5000) concurrently with `Promise.all` wherever they don't depend on each other.

## 2. Detailed error handling + fallback strategies

```js
const CircuitBreaker = require('opossum');

async function rawCall(params) {
  const { data } = await client.post('/translate', params);
  return data;
}

async function callWithRetry(params, attempt = 0) {
  try {
    return await breaker.fire(params);
  } catch (err) {
    const status = err.response?.status;
    const retryable = !status || status === 429 || status >= 500 || err.code === 'ECONNABORTED';
    if (retryable && attempt < 3) {
      const wait = 2 ** attempt * 100 + Math.random() * 100;  // exp. backoff + jitter
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, wait));
      return callWithRetry(params, attempt + 1);
    }
    if (status === 403) throw new Error('DeepL auth failed — check DEEPL_KEY');   // don't retry
    if (status === 456) throw new Error('DeepL quota exhausted for the period');  // don't retry
    throw err;
  }
}

const breaker = new CircuitBreaker(rawCall, {
  timeout: 2000, errorThresholdPercentage: 50, resetTimeout: 30000,
});
```

Fallback ladder, in order — each step keeps the chatbot answering:

1. **Stale cache**: on failure, re-read the key ignoring freshness (store a second copy at a long TTL, e.g. `tr:stale:*` for 24h).
2. **Original text**: return the untranslated string on 5xx, as you set up for the DeepL 500 case. The user sees their own language, not an error.
3. **Default message**: for UI strings, a default greeting rather than a blank.
4. **Queue it**: push non-interactive work (history back-translation) to the bull queue and reply immediately.
5. **Open circuit**: while open, skip the network entirely and serve from step 1/2 — this is what stops a DeepL outage from turning 180ms into 2000ms timeouts across every request.

Always log status, `error.code`, target language, and text length via Winston; never log the full text if it is clinical content.

## 3. Metrics to capture

**API response time statistics** (per endpoint, exported to Prometheus/Grafana):

| Metric | Why |
|---|---|
| Mean | Overall trend vs. your 300ms SLA |
| Median | Typical experience, unskewed by outliers |
| Standard deviation | Variability/jitter in response times |
| Min | Best case — effectively your cache-hit path |
| Max | Worst case, usually a retry or timeout |
| 95th percentile | The number to hold under 300ms; 1 in 20 users is worse than this |

```js
const metrics = {
  times: [], hits: 0, misses: 0,
  record(ms) { this.times.push(ms); },
  hit() { this.hits++; }, miss() { this.misses++; },
  report() {
    const s = [...this.times].sort((a, b) => a - b), n = s.length;
    const mean = s.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / n;
    const sd = Math.sqrt(s.reduce((a, b) => a + (b - mean) ** 2, 0) / n);
    return {
      mean: +mean.toFixed(2),
      median: s[Math.floor(n / 2)],
      stdDev: +sd.toFixed(2),
      min: s[0], max: s[n - 1],
      p95: s[Math.floor(n * 0.95)],
      cacheHitRate: +(this.hits / (this.hits + this.misses) * 100).toFixed(1),
      hits: this.hits, misses: this.misses,
    };
  },
};
```

**Cache hit rate statistics**: hits, misses, hit rate, and hit rate split by target language and by TTL bucket. You are at **85%** on translations; the session-metadata cache is the weak one at **70%**. In Grafana: `rate(cache_hits[5m]) / (rate(cache_hits[5m]) + rate(cache_misses[5m]))`. Raising 85% → 92% is worth roughly 45% fewer DeepL calls.

**Latency improvements to expect**, against your measured ~180ms average:

| Change | Effect |
|---|---|
| Redis cache, 15-min TTL (in place) | 85% hit rate; already cut API calls ~30% |
| Batching into one DeepL call | Removes per-string round-trips; large win on multi-string renders |
| HTTP keep-alive / connection pooling | Saves the ~20-40ms handshake per call |
| Parallel detection + translation + GPT-4 | Total ≈ slowest leg instead of the sum |
| Webhook instead of polling (in place) | Polling down 60% |
| Circuit breaker | Caps failure-path latency at ~resetTimeout instead of full timeouts |
| CDN / edge cache for static UI strings | Serves repeat phrases without touching 4500 |
| GIN index work on the JSONB session table | Target 120ms → 30ms on the memory lookups feeding this |

One thing still open from before: you never confirmed which behaviour you want when the detected language isn't supported by DeepL — fall back to English, or re-detect. The ladder above returns the original text in that case, which for a psychology context is probably safer than silently showing English; tell me which you prefer and I'll wire it in.

Think of the cache as the fast, automatic path and DeepL as the effortful one — the goal is that most requests never need the effortful path, and that when it stalls, the user still gets a coherent reply rather than a blank.
